Yes — but only on MongoDB. Prisma calls this a **composite type** (embedded document). Define it with `type` instead of `model`, then reference it as a field:

```prisma
type Address {
  country String
}

model User {
  id      String  @id @default(auto()) @map("_id") @db.ObjectId
  name    String
  address Address
}
```

This has no separate collection — it's embedded directly in the `User` document, unlike a `@relation`, which does create a separate collection.

Source: `apps/docs/content/docs/orm/v7/prisma-client/special-fields-and-types/composite-types.mdx` ("Composite types" — MongoDB-only warning + example schema).

